// The ShapeType of the OMG `dds-rtps` interoperability test suite —
// `srcCxx/shape.idl`, pinned suite 526350d3.
//
// THERE ARE TWO ShapeTypes UNDER THIS NAME, and this is one of them.
// The other is `shapesdemo.idl` beside this file: `@final`, four
// members. Read `README.md` here before you use either.
//
// Who speaks this one
//   The executables of the OMG `dds-rtps` suite — the RTI, CycloneDDS,
//   Fast DDS, OpenDDS and ZeroDDS shape mains that the suite drives
//   against each other — and `tools/omg-shape-main` on our side.
//
// Why `@appendable` and not `@final`
//   Because the suite's IDL says so, and the difference reaches the
//   wire: XTypes 1.3 Tab. 59 gives an `@appendable` aggregate
//   DELIMITED_CDR under XCDR2, which is a leading `uint32` DHEADER
//   (XTypes 1.3 §7.4.3.4.2) and the encapsulation id `D_CDR2_LE`
//   (`0x00 0x09`) instead of `CDR2_LE` (`0x00 0x07`). Handing a
//   `@final` body to one of these peers is not a smaller sample, it is
//   a mis-framed one: CycloneDDS's appendable reader takes
//   `color.length` for the DHEADER and reports
//   `deserialization Square/ShapeType failed`.
//
// Why `uint8` and not `octet`
//   Because the suite's IDL says `sequence<uint8>`, and the two are
//   different types: XTypes 1.3 Table 25 maps IDL `uint8` to UInt8
//   (TK_UINT8) and `octet` to Byte (TK_BYTE), and Table 15 makes a
//   primitive type assignable from the same primitive type only. A
//   peer that announces the suite's type in its TypeObject is not
//   assignable from a `sequence<octet>` writer: Intercom DDS 4.3.1
//   announces TK_UINT8, and this side refused the match. The octets on
//   the wire are the same.
//
// Why the fifth member is always on the wire
//   `additional_payload_size` carries its size in its *length*; the
//   octets are filler for the suite's large-data and throughput runs
//   (`--additional-payload-size`). A normal shape sample writes length
//   zero — it is written, not omitted, so that the wire type stays the
//   five-member one.
//
// What checks it
//   `crates/dcps/tests/shapes_type_idl_agreement.rs` encodes the same
//   values through this file and through the hand-written
//   `crates/dcps/src/interop.rs` and compares the octets, in all four
//   modes the matrix carries.

@extensibility(APPENDABLE)
struct ShapeType {
    @key string<128> color;
    long x;
    long y;
    long shapesize;
    sequence<uint8> additional_payload_size;
};
